# <%= it.scope.slice(1) %>-remotes

One or more remotes made with [mfeflow](https://github.com/mfeflow/mfeflow). Each one exposes `./App` and is
deployed on its own. The shell that loads them lives in another repo and is owned by someone else.

```
apps/<remote>   one micro-frontend each, exposing ./App
mfeflow.json    what the CLI generated; `repo: "remote"` means this repo has no shell
```

There is no `registry/` folder here. Which build of a remote each environment serves is recorded in the shell's repo.

## Run it

```sh
pnpm install
pnpm dev
```

`pnpm dev` starts every remote on its own port. `pnpm mfeflow dev` does the same with a prefixed log per app,
and picks up a remote you add without a restart.

To see a remote inside the shell, open the shell's page with `?mfe-override=<remote>@http://localhost:<port>/mf-manifest.json`.
That works only if the shell is a development build; production builds ignore the parameter.

## Change it

```sh
pnpm mfeflow add remote checkout     # a new remote; no route or registry entry, those are in the shell's repo
pnpm mfeflow add <feature>           # see `pnpm mfeflow features`
```

A feature that also changes the shell (a theme provider, translations) is applied to the remotes here. Add it in the
shell's repo as well.

## Check it

```sh
pnpm doctor              # config, singletons, drift; compatibility when the shell address is known
pnpm validate            # mfeflow.json and every mfeflow.app.json
pnpm check-compat        # each remote's shared ranges against the deployed shell
pnpm budget              # after `pnpm build`: first-load JS per remote
```

The shell's address is the `shell` field of `mfeflow.json` (set with `mfeflow init --shell`). Without it, `doctor` warns that the
shell is not in this repo; pass `--against <url|file>` to check against one.
